<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#meroitic-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meroitic script</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meroitic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meroitic script was the writing system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> in its Meroitic period, used from about the second century BCE. It existed in hieroglyphic and cursive forms derived from Egyptian writing. Although its signs can be read, the Meroitic language remains only partly understood, and the script is among the Kushite Empire's distinctive legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroitic-script.jpg" alt="Meroitic script" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meroitic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Meroitic script consists of two alphasyllabic scripts developed to write the Meroitic language at the beginning of the Meroitic Period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>. Meroitic Cursive was derived from Demotic Egyptian, and Meroitic Hieroglyphs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a>. The cursive is the most widely attested, making up about 90 per cent of inscriptions, and it predates the earliest surviving hieroglyphic inscription by a century or more. Before the Meroitic Period, Egyptian hieroglyphs were used to write Kushite names and words. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> described the two scripts in his Bibliotheca historica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>There were 23 letters, including four vowels. The consonants were understood to carry an inherent vowel a, so that a single letter m was read ma. The other vowels were written in full, so that mi stood for the syllable mi. The four signs ne, se, te and to stood for consonants with other vowels. The cursive was written from right to left and from top to bottom, while the monumental form ran in columns from top to bottom, right to left, with the signs facing the beginning of the text. The only punctuation mark was a divider of two to three dots. Some scholars, such as Harald Haarmann, believe that the vowel letters show an influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The script was deciphered by the British Egyptologist Francis Llewellyn Griffith from the Meroitic spellings of Egyptian names, but the Meroitic language itself remains poorly understood. Later scholars, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a> and Rowan, proposed other readings of the sign e and the signs ne, se and te. In late 2008 the first complete royal dedication was found. Both scripts were added to Unicode in 2012.</p><p>The last known inscription is the Meroitic Cursive text of the Blemmye king Kharamadoye on a column in the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalabsha">Kalabsha</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> the script continued in use for a time, and during the 6th century Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> it was replaced by Byzantine Greek, Coptic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a>. Old Nubian added three Meroitic Cursive letters to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meroitic script” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They also developed their own <b>Meroitic script</b>, a sign of growing cultural independence from Egypt; its signs can be read, but its language is still only partly understood.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Nubia, conquered by the New Kingdom pharaohs, Egyptian became the language of temples and government, and the kings of Kush who ruled Egypt in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE wrote in Egyptian hieroglyphs; the later <b>Meroitic script</b> of Kush was derived from Egyptian signs.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
